<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hand-cannon">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hand cannon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as hand cannons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The hand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> was an early firearm consisting of a simple metal tube fired by igniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> by hand. It developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and spread widely in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> equipped its armies with hand cannons as part of its large-scale use of gunpowder weapons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hand-cannon.jpg" alt="Hand cannon" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sven Rosborn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gun_Sweden_Morko.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The hand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, also called the gonne or handgonne, is regarded as the first true firearm and the successor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-lance">fire lance</a>. It is the simplest metal-barrelled firearm, and unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> it has no firing mechanism and is ignited by hand through a touch hole. In 1259 a fire-emitting lance made of a large bamboo tube appeared, which, according to the History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, spewed out a pellet wad with a sound heard five hundred or more paces away. Fire lances then changed from bamboo, wood or paper barrels to metal ones.</p><p>A rock sculpture at the Dazu Rock Carvings, dated to 1128, may show a hand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, but scholars dispute this. Hand cannons saw widespread use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> during the 13th century. The History of Yuan reports that in 1287 troops under Li Ting used them against the Mongol prince Nayan, causing great confusion among the enemy. The oldest extant dated hand cannon is the Xanadu Gun, whose era date corresponds to 1298.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest reliable evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> is a register of the municipality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1326, and by 1338 hand cannons were in wide use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Loshult gun, a Swedish example of about 10 kg from the mid-14th century, is among the oldest survivors. In the Islamic world the textual evidence is debated: metal-barrel cannon are securely described from 1365.</p><p>Cannon are attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1366. Goryeo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> learned of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by 1372 and produced hand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> by 1377, and Đại Việt soldiers used them by 1390 to kill the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> king Che Bong Nga. Japan was aware of gunpowder warfare from the Mongol invasions, but firearms were not produced there until the Portuguese introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a> in 1543.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A hand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> consists of a barrel, a handle and sometimes a socket for a wooden stock. It could be held in two hands, with a second person igniting it with smouldering wood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, red-hot iron rods or slow matches, or rested and fired by one gunner. Early projectiles were rocks, pebbles and arrows, then stone balls, which were replaced by iron balls from the late 14th to the 15th century. Later versions gained a flash pan, and then a hinged metal lid to keep the priming dry. In 15th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, corned powder, shot ammunition and the flash pan led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebus</a>, the first firearm with a trigger.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hand cannon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Infantry used crossbows, pikes and gunpowder weapons including cannon and firearms such as <b>hand cannons</b>, while cavalry provided mobility and rapid response.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>One of the oldest surviving guns, a small bronze <b>hand cannon</b> found in Heilongjiang, is dated by the circumstances of its discovery to around 1288.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>Armed with <b>hand cannons</b>, Vietnamese rebels under Lê Lợi ambushed and routed a Ming army under General Wang Tong between the villages of Tốt Động and Chúc Động, near Hanoi.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
